
Pointer

Definition
    * A pointer is a variable that stores the memory address of another variable.
    * It “points” to a location in memory where data resides.
    * On a 64‑bit system, a pointer always occupies 8 bytes (4 bytes on 32‑bit).

Declaration
    * Syntax          :  data_type *pointer_name;
    * Example         :  int *p;          // p can hold the address of an int
    * & (address‑of)  :  gives the address of a variable.
    * * (dereference) :  accesses the value stored at the address held by the pointer.

Memory & Address
    * A normal variable has two properties: its address and its value.
    * A pointer variable has two properties: its own address and the address it stores.
      int num = 10;
      int *p = &num;

         p      →  address of num           (e.g., 0x7ffe14c2ee54)
        *p      →  value at that address    (10)
        &p      →  address of p itself      (e.g., 0x7ffe14c2e200)

Pointer Arithmetic
    * p + n  moves the pointer forward by  n * sizeof(*p)  bytes.
    * p++    points to the next element of the base type.
    * Valid operations  :  pointer + int, pointer – int, pointer – pointer (same array).
    * Array indexing is pointer arithmetic  :  arr[i]   ≡  *(arr + i).

Pointers and Arrays
    * The name of an array acts as a constant pointer to its first element.
         int arr[5];
         arr    == &arr[0]
         arr+i  == &arr[i]
         *(arr+i) == arr[i]
    * Can traverse an array using a pointer:
         int *p = arr;
         for(i = 0; i < n; i++)
             printf("%d ", *(p + i));
    * sizeof(array)  gives total bytes of the array;
      sizeof(pointer) gives only the size of the pointer variable (e.g., 8 bytes).

Types of Pointers
    * Null pointer       : points nowhere (int *p = NULL;). Safe to check before use.
    * Void pointer       : (void *) can store the address of any data type;
                           must be explicitly cast before dereferencing.
    * Wild pointer       : uninitialised pointer; dereferencing causes undefined behaviour.
    * Dangling pointer   : points to memory that has been freed or has gone out of scope.
    * Pointer to pointer : stores the address of another pointer.
         int **q = &p;   // q → p → data

Pointers and Functions
    * Pass‑by‑reference : send the address of a variable to allow the function to modify it.
         void swap(int *a, int *b) 
         {
             int temp = *a;
             *a = *b;
             *b = temp;
         }
    * When an array is passed, only the base address is sent (the function receives a pointer).
    * Returning a pointer from a function is possible, but never return the address of a local variable.

Pointers and Strings
    * Strings in C are null‑terminated character arrays.
         char *ptr = "Hello";      // ptr points to a read‑only string literal
         char str[] = "Hello";     // str is a modifiable copy of the literal
    * Traversal:  while(*ptr) { /* use *ptr */ ptr++; }

Dynamic Memory Allocation
    * Functions in <stdlib.h>:  malloc, calloc, realloc, free.
         int *p = (int*)malloc(n * sizeof(int));
         // use p …
         free(p);
    * Always check the returned pointer against NULL.
    * Forgetting to free leads to memory leaks.

Advantages
    * Efficient handling of arrays and strings.
    * Enables dynamic memory management.
    * Allows functions to modify actual arguments.
    * Foundation for complex data structures (linked lists, trees, etc.).

Limitations
    * Uninitialised or dangling pointers cause crashes / undefined behaviour.
    * Manual memory management risks memory leaks.
    * No automatic bounds checking on pointer arithmetic.
    * Syntax can be confusing and error‑prone.



