1. Basic Pointer Assignment
   Problem : Declare an integer variable, assign it a value, then declare a pointer that holds its address.
            Print the address stored in the pointer, the value using the pointer, and the address of the pointer itself.
   Solution:
        int num = 42;
        int *ptr = &num;

        printf("Value of num         : %d\n", num);
        printf("Address of num       : %p\n", (void*)&num);
        printf("Pointer ptr stores   : %p\n", (void*)ptr);
        printf("Value via *ptr       : %d\n", *ptr);
        printf("Address of ptr itself: %p\n", (void*)&ptr);

2. Double Pointer
   Problem : Create an integer, a pointer to it, and a pointer to that pointer. Access the original value using the double pointer.
   Solution:
        int x = 99;
        int *p  = &x;
        int **q = &p;

        printf("%d\n", **q);   // prints 99

3. Pointer Arithmetic – Traversing an Array
   Problem : Create an array of 5 integers. Use a pointer to traverse and print all elements without using array subscript [ ].
   Solution:
        int arr[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
        int *p = arr;
        for(int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            printf("%d ", *(p + i));
        }

4. Input into an Array Using a Pointer
   Problem : Accept 5 integers into an array using a pointer (instead of &arr[i]).
   Solution:
        int arr[5];
        int *p = arr;
        for(int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
            scanf("%d", p + i);   // equivalent to &arr[i]
        }
        for(int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
