Dynamic Memory Allocation

Definition
    * Dynamic Memory Allocation (DMA) allows a program to request memory during runtime.
    * Memory is allocated from the heap segment.
    * The programmer is responsible for releasing the memory when it is no longer needed.
    * Contrasts with static/automatic memory (stack), which is handled automatically at compile time.

Need
    * When the exact amount of memory required is not known until runtime.
    * To create data structures that can grow or shrink (linked lists, trees, dynamic arrays).
    * Memory persists beyond the scope of the function where it was allocated (until freed).

Memory Layout of a C Program
    * Text / Code segment    : compiled machine instructions (read-only).
    * Initialized data segment: global/static variables with explicit initial values (e.g., int a = 10;).
    * Uninitialized data (BSS): global/static variables without initializers (e.g., int a;); zeroed on startup.
    * Stack                   : local variables, function call frames. Managed automatically (LIFO).
    * Heap                    : memory allocated at runtime via malloc, calloc, realloc. Managed manually.

Stack vs Heap
    * Stack                          Heap
      – Automatic allocation          – Manual allocation (malloc, etc.)
      – Fast access                   – Slower access
      – Limited size                  – Larger memory pool
      – No fragmentation              – Can become fragmented
      – Lifetime tied to scope        – Lifetime controlled by programmer
      – Direct access (variable name) – Access only through pointers

Dynamic Memory Functions (stdlib.h)
    * malloc(size_t size)
        – Allocates 'size' bytes of uninitialised memory.
        – Returns void*; cast to desired type.
        – On failure returns NULL.
        – Syntax: ptr = (type*)malloc(count * sizeof(type));

    * calloc(size_t count, size_t size)
        – Allocates memory for 'count' elements, each of 'size' bytes.
        – Initialises all bytes to zero.
        – Returns void*; cast to desired type.
        – Syntax: ptr = (type*)calloc(count, sizeof(type));

    * realloc(void *ptr, size_t new_size)
        – Resizes previously allocated memory.
        – May move the block to a new location.
        – Contents up to min(old_size, new_size) are preserved.
        – If ptr is NULL, behaves like malloc(new_size).
        – If new_size is 0, behaviour is implementation-defined (may free or not; better to use free).
        – Syntax: ptr = (type*)realloc(ptr, new_count * sizeof(type));

    * free(void *ptr)
        – Releases memory previously allocated by malloc/calloc/realloc.
        – After freeing, set the pointer to NULL to avoid dangling pointer.
        – Never free memory that has not been dynamically allocated.
        – Never free the same block twice.

Rules for Safe DMA Usage
    * Always check the returned pointer against NULL before using.
    * Initialise allocated memory (calloc does this automatically; malloc does not).
    * After free(ptr), set ptr = NULL; to prevent accidental use.
    * Do not dereference a pointer after it has been freed (dangling pointer).
    * Do not free the same memory twice; double free causes undefined behaviour.
    * Always free memory when it is no longer needed to avoid memory leaks.
    * Do not use memory beyond the allocated size (buffer overflow).

Advantages
    * Flexible memory usage: allocate exactly what is needed at runtime.
    * Enables complex and dynamic data structures.
    * Data can outlive the creating function.
    * Reduces wastage when maximum needed size is unpredictable.

Limitations
    * Manual management is error-prone (leaks, double frees, dangling pointers).
    * Slower than stack allocation.
    * Fragmentation may lead to inefficient memory use.
    * Forgetting to free leads to memory exhaustion in long-running programs.



